autonomic-agent (0.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Perception goes through the channel. Until now the agent was a heartbeat
    and a version ledger: it knew its own uid and cgroup limits, and nothing
    at all about the machine around it. Not because nobody thought to look --
    because it cannot: agentd runs under ProtectSystem=strict and
    ProtectProc=invisible, so /proc and /sys are physically out of reach.
    There were two ways to fix that: loosen the sandbox (that is adding
    *power*) or have the privileged side read on its behalf (that is adding
    *capability*). This release does the second, which makes perception a
    primitive like any other -- same socket, same audit trail.
    - sys.read(metric): a closed set of nine metrics -- os.release, kernel,
      hostname, uptime, loadavg, meminfo, cpuinfo_count, disk_usage,
      timezone. The caller supplies a *metric name*, never a path. A sys.read
      that took a path would be an arbitrary-file-read hole (/etc/shadow is
      also a file), and a channel that can read anything is harder to audit
      than one that can write a named thing, because writes are fenced by an
      allowlist and reads quietly become "exfiltrate the machine".
    - service.status(unit): active / enabled / substate, from systemctl's
      machine-readable output. Unit names reject '-'-prefixed and path-like
      values, since systemctl would otherwise treat them as its own options.
    - Read-only primitives are always changed=False and never bump
      action_seq. The world version number exists to answer "has this machine
      changed since I last looked"; a read that moved it would make that
      answer permanently "yes", and the ledger would degrade into a visit
      counter.
    - autonomicctl sense: one snapshot, every read through the channel, then
      projected read-only to /run/autonomic/sense (0444) together with the
      world version at the moment of reading. Metrics that cannot be read are
      still written into the snapshot, marked unavailable with a reason --
      a snapshot that is quietly missing a few lines looks like everything is
      fine, while one that says so is telling the truth. The wording keeps
      "this machine does not have that file" distinct from "the sandbox
      blocked me": collapsing the two gives a confidently wrong answer.
    - Why the metric list is hardcoded on the caller's side rather than asked
      of actd: asking for "whatever you have" is a drifting target -- adding
      a primitive would silently change what a snapshot contains, and
      snapshots taken before and after would no longer be comparable. If the
      two sides disagree, that is a gap in the chain, and it should be seen
      rather than swallowed.

  * Same lesson as M8's third bug, applied again. pwd is POSIX-only, so
    user.add could never exercise its happy path off Linux. The new
    primitives walked straight into it: os.statvfs does not exist on Windows
    either, and the failure surfaced as an AttributeError rather than a
    reading. Platform-specific calls are now wrapped in small replaceable
    functions, and "this platform has no statvfs" is reported differently
    from "statvfs failed" -- the first tells you to check the platform, the
    second to check permissions. Same treatment for three more of the same
    kind: autonomicctl no longer explodes at import time on a platform
    without grp/pwd; _act_call reports a missing AF_UNIX as a readable
    sentence instead of an AttributeError; and a failed projection no longer
    discards what was already read.

  * Self-checks: 137 assertions across four suites. test-sense.py is new and
    is deliberate about what it does *not* assert -- not the values (they
    differ per machine), but the shape of the path: every read goes through
    the channel, gaps are recorded rather than omitted, and nothing read-only
    ever reaches an apply phase.

 -- LiGuiyu <openclaw@liguiyu.com>  Sat, 11 Oct 2026 22:40:00 +0000

autonomic-agent (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * More primitives, not more power. The channel grows from 5 actions to 9,
    and not one constraint was loosened to make that happen.
    - pkg.install / pkg.remove / pkg.list: apt. This is the first action that
      *executes code other people wrote* -- a Debian postinst runs as root --
      so package names are held to the Debian character set, may not start
      with '-', and may not pin a version. "--allow-unauthenticated" would
      otherwise be eaten by apt as its own option, and a pinned version is a
      way to choose one with a known hole. Five packages per action, max:
      the blast radius lives in code, not in the caller's restraint.
    - pkg.list is the read-only one: an LLM planning to install nginx should
      be able to ask whether apache is already there first.
    - dry_run runs `apt-get -s` so apt itself computes the dependency tree,
      and verify asks dpkg-query what the status is *now* rather than
      believing what apt said.
    - user.add: the first irreversible primitive, and the first thing that
      makes --force-irreversible actually do something. Deleting a user does
      not take its files, its running services, or its UID recorded in other
      config along with it, so declaring this reversible would be a lie. The
      extra pause that flag buys is the safety mechanism, not a formality.
  * scripts/test-primitives.py: 39 assertions over the argument guards, all
    pure functions, now part of CI.

 -- Autonomic OS <autonomic@localhost>  Sat, 11 Oct 2026 04:00:00 +0000

autonomic-agent (0.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Planned action: plain words in, audited actions out. This is the missing
    piece between the LLM and the Action channel -- until now the channel
    existed but nothing translated intent into calls it accepts.
    - `autonomicctl ask "..."`: the model proposes, the channel disposes
    - the tool list handed to the model is fetched live from autonomic-actd,
      never hardcoded in the planner, so the two cannot drift apart
    - every plan must pass structural checks (valid JSON, known action, known
      parameters, path inside the write allowlist) and every step must carry
      a reason; an unexplained write is refused
    - all steps are dry_run'd before ANY step is applied, because a
      half-applied plan leaves a machine that is neither where it was nor
      where you asked it to go
    - each step is verified immediately after apply; on failure everything
      stops and the already-applied steps are reported with their backups
  * Reversibility becomes a fact instead of a claim: backups now carry a
    sidecar recording where the file came from (the filename alone is
    ambiguous, since "_" is legal in Linux paths), and the new `file.restore`
    primitive puts them back. A reversible flag with no way back is a wish,
    not a capability.
  * `autonomicctl backups` lists every file the channel overwrote and prints
    the exact command to restore each one.
  * Backup filenames now carry microseconds: within the same second a second
    write silently overwrote the only copy of the previous contents.
  * scripts/mock-llm.py ships a fake OpenAI-compatible endpoint so the whole
    AI path can be verified without a real API key, and repeatedly.

 -- Autonomic OS <autonomic@localhost>  Sat, 11 Oct 2026 02:00:00 +0800

autonomic-agent (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Power switch. The agent can now hold credentials for an OpenAI-compatible
    endpoint and tell you whether it actually works:
    - /etc/autonomic/agent.conf (0644) holds base_url/model/timeout
    - /etc/autonomic/credentials.env (0640 root:autonomic) holds the key only
    - the unit injects it with systemd LoadCredential=, so the secret never
      enters the process environment and cannot be grepped out of the journal
    - `autonomicctl setup` writes both (interactive, or --non-interactive for
      CI) and probes GET /models immediately
    - `autonomicctl llm` shows the config and re-checks connectivity
  * At boot the daemon now reports whether it has a usable credential, without
    calling anything. An unconfigured machine is still Autonomic OS -- it just
    has identity without a mind.
  * The Action channel (CHARTER 1.5), which is the reason any of the above
    matters. A key is useless if the agent cannot do anything with what it
    decides.
    The agent is deliberately NOT root. It now has a privileged companion,
    autonomic-actd, reachable only over a unix socket (0660 root:autonomic)
    whose peer credentials are read with SO_PEERCRED before the request is
    even parsed. There is no way to reduce this to a shell: four primitives
    (file.write, service.set, sysctl.set, self.test), each with a parameter
    schema and each gated by a write allowlist that refuses /etc/shadow,
    sudoers, ssh host keys and their neighbours outright.
  * Every action answers three questions instead of promising one:
    dry_run (what would change, touching nothing), apply (do it, journal it),
    verify (go look at the world again). `autonomicctl act` refuses to touch
    anything it has not shown you first, and always re-checks afterwards.
    When verify disagrees with apply, verify wins.
  * Every successful apply signals the counter daemon, so action_seq is now
    a real ledger of real changes rather than a decorative counter. Things
    done outside this channel do not move it -- that difference is precisely
    what drift detection is for.
  * No new runtime dependencies: urllib from the standard library only.

